Cyril Lucaris and his Confession (1629):
**Cyril Lucaris** (1572–1638) is the most tragic figure in the Orthodox encounter with Protestantism: a patriarch who apparently published a **Calvinist confession of faith** in the name of the Orthodox Church.

**His life.** Born in **Crete** (then Venetian), he studied in Venice and Padua, and travelled in Poland, where he saw the Union of Brest (Stage 5, Lesson 22) and the Jesuit missions. He came to see **Rome as the great danger** to the Orthodox. He became **Patriarch of Alexandria (1601–1620)**, then **Patriarch of Constantinople (1620)**, deposed and restored several times by the Ottoman authorities, as Jesuit-backed and Protestant-backed factions bribed the Porte.

**His Protestant friendships.** Lucaris corresponded with Dutch Calvinists and with the Archbishop of Canterbury, George Abbot. He sent young Greeks to study in Geneva and England. He sent the ***Codex Alexandrinus***, one of the oldest manuscripts of the Greek Bible, to **King Charles I** of England (1627). He backed a Greek printing press in Constantinople, destroyed in 1628 at the instigation of his Jesuit opponents.

**The Confession.** In **1629** a ***Confession of Faith*** in Latin, in Lucaris's name, was published in **Geneva**; a Greek edition followed in 1633, with four added questions and answers. In eighteen chapters it taught, among other things:
- **Scripture alone** as the rule, above the Church;
- **unconditional predestination**;
- **justification by faith alone**;
- **two sacraments** only (baptism and the Eucharist), and a Calvinist (spiritual) presence;
- that the Church **can err**;
- rejection of the **veneration of icons**, though allowing images.

**Was it his?** The Synod of Jerusalem (1672) argued that it was a **forgery** by Calvinists, citing his Orthodox sermons. **Most historians today, Orthodox included, accept that he wrote it**, or at least approved it; his letters to Protestants say similar things. Why he did it is disputed: genuine conversion to Calvinism; a strategy to win Protestant support against Rome and reform an uneducated clergy; or both.

**His death.** On **27 June 1638**, accused by his enemies of stirring up the Cossacks against the Sultan, Lucaris was **strangled by janissaries** on the order of Sultan Murad IV, and his body thrown into the Bosphorus. Some Protestants regard him as a martyr. **Some Orthodox remember his zeal for his flock and his opposition to the Unia with sympathy, while rejecting his Confession.**

**Their case for him.** Protestants then and now argue: an Orthodox patriarch, a learned man who knew the Fathers, found in the Reformed faith the faith of the early Church. If the Orthodox were honest, they would follow him.

**The Orthodox answer.** **One patriarch is not the Church.** Patriarchs have been heretics before (Nestorius, Sergius of Constantinople). The Church judged the Confession at councils of **Constantinople (1638, 1642)**, **Iasi (1642)** and **Jerusalem (1672)** (Lesson 21), and found that it contradicted the faith of the Fathers on every point where it followed Geneva. **The reception of the Church, not a single bishop's signature, tells what is Orthodox.**

**History told honestly.** Lucaris's fight was in part against **real Jesuit pressure** to Latinise the Orthodox, and the Orthodox replies to him drew, in their turn, on Latin theology. Florovsky called the whole episode a sign of the Orthodox Church's lack of its own theological schools: **Orthodox in that century had to choose weapons from one Western armoury or the other.**
